zfp -- vendored into SZ3
========================

Upstream:  https://github.com/LLNL/zfp, release 1.0.1 (CODEC 5)
License:   BSD-3-Clause (see LICENSE in this directory)

Vendored: the macro-templated block codec (src/template/), the bitstream, and the headers
those need, in upstream's own directory layout. Not taken: the array classes, the C API,
the execution policies, the GPU backends -- SZ3 drives the block codec directly, via
zfp_codec.hpp.

Changes to upstream files
-------------------------

1. Three include directives were made relative: zfp/bitstream.h names internal/zfp/types.h
   and internal/zfp/system.h, zfp/bitstream.inl names bitstream.h. Upstream writes them
   against its own include root, which SZ3 does not put on the include path.

2. Six include guards gained an SZ3_ prefix, so SZ3 and an installed zfp can be used
   together: ZFP_TYPES_H, ZFP_BITSTREAM_H, ZFP_INLINE_H, ZFP_MACROS_H and ZFP_SYSTEM_H
   became SZ3_ZFP_ZFP_*, and TEMPLATE_H became SZ3_ZFP_TEMPLATE_H.

3. zfp/bitstream.h gained `struct bitstream;` before the typedef of the same name. This
   header is included inside a namespace, where that typedef would otherwise alias an
   enclosing-scope `bitstream` -- such as an installed zfp's -- and then collide with it.

4. zfp/bitstream.h has `extern_ const size_t stream_word_bits;` commented out. Upstream
   defines it in bitstream.c, which is not vendored; `extern_` is `extern "C"`, so the
   declaration escaped the namespace and would bind to an installed libzfp. Unused here.

Nothing else was changed.

Things to know before upgrading
-------------------------------

zfp_codec.hpp compensates for two upstream properties rather than editing the sources:
it defines `inline_` before inline.h runs (upstream keys it off __STDC_VERSION__, which
C++ never defines), and it puts each instantiation in an anonymous namespace (upstream's
public block entry points are not static).

Three upstream behaviours differ from SZ3's contracts. All are zfp's own, and all reproduce
in upstream's build; Config::absErrorBound is otherwise a hard guarantee in SZ3.

  - A block is coded against a single exponent, so the error bottoms out at about 2^-25 of
    the block maximum for float and 2^-57 for double, whatever bound is asked. This is not
    confined to blocks of wide dynamic range: a smooth order-1 float field stops improving
    at 8.9e-8, so no bound below roughly 3e-8 is met. Nothing warns.

  - Float data below about 2^-97 in magnitude misses a bound under about 4e-28: the scale
    factor the block-floating-point cast forms overflows to infinity. Measured at 3x the
    bound for 1e-30 data at a 1e-30 bound, upstream included.

  - NaN and infinities are not preserved, and are not reported either: they come back as
    finite numbers. Which numbers is target-dependent, since converting an out-of-range
    float to an integer is undefined; on arm64, NaN as 0 and either infinity as -2.

Citation: P. Lindstrom, "Fixed-Rate Compressed Floating-Point Arrays," IEEE TVCG 20(12), 2014.
